    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d49">

                <pb facs="0138" n="116" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg116"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">886A.2553/11–2250: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Holmes</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-11-22T19:00:00+00:00">November 22,
                            1950—7 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3022. Furlonge<anchor n="1" xml:id="d49-f1"/> said today Foreign Office now
                    drawing up reply to Saudi note of September 22 and present plan is to take more
                    lenient line than heretofore. He said UK anxious avoid further delay in
                    establishing fact-finding commission. He indicated Foreign Office might modify
                    its request that Saudis provide statement explaining basis their claims by
                    waiving this requirement on claims prior 1938. He said also that presentation
                    reply would probably be handled somewhat differently by instructing Trott
                    deliver note to King in Riyadh.</p>
                <p>Furlonge said Foreign Office attached importance obtaining fuller justification
                    Saudi claims at least since 1938 because (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)
                    Foreign Office thought they were completely unreasonable and (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) it felt UK members commission needed clearer
                    idea Saudi pretensions before they were competent begin investigation.</p>
                <p>Cranston in another conversation today said UK wished be accommodating as
                    possible and he otherwise also made clear UK considering softer line.</p>
                <p>Cranston said that oil survey on Trucial coast in area claimed by SAG had now been completed and camp to which Saudis
                    had protested had been removed. Only operations in this area now are from
                    launches off coast.</p>
                <pb facs="0139" n="117" xml:id="pg117"/>
                <p>Trott has informed Foreign Office of Saudi displeasure over UK attitude as
                    conveyed in Riyadh to US Ambassador (Jidda’s 318, November 20).<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d49-f2"/> Cranston said this information was being taken into
                    consideration in preparation of UK reply to September 22 note.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 3022, repeated information Jidda 22.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Holmes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
